# [PLENARY](../wg/plenary.html)

## Summary

The IETF 127 Plenary session was held in San Francisco, California, facilitated by IETF Chair Roman Danyliw. The session marked the 40th anniversary of the first IETF meeting in 1986, recognizing founding participants Bob Hinden, Mike StJohns, and Lixia Zhang. The meeting featured host remarks from VeriSign, the presentation of the 2026 Jonathan B. Postel Service Award to the late Dave Taht, operational and leadership updates from the IESG, IAB, IRTF, NomCom, IETF Trust / IPMC, and the IETF LLC, followed by open mic sessions for the IAB, IESG, and IETF LLC Board.

---

## Key Discussion Points

### 1. 40th Anniversary Recognition and Session Poll
- **40th Anniversary:** Roman Danyliw recognized forty years of IETF history since IETF 1 in San Diego (1986). Original participants Bob Hinden, Mike StJohns, and Lixia Zhang were recognized for their long-standing contributions.
- **Participation:** Over 900 on-site participants and over 900 remote participants checked in for IETF 127, making it the highest attended meeting in the last two years.
- **Session Poll:**
  1. How many IETF meetings have you participated in, either remotely or in person? — This is my first!: 23, 2–15 meetings: 86, 16–30 meetings: 86, 50+ meetings: 55 (total: 296)

### 2. Host Welcome & Jonathan B. Postel Service Award
- **Host Remarks:** Bert Kaliski (SVP and CTO of VeriSign) welcomed attendees and reiterated VeriSign's commitment to supporting open Internet standards. Roman Danyliw presented VeriSign with a token of appreciation.
- **Postel Award:** Brian Haberman (Chair of the Internet Society Board of Trustees) presented the 2026 Jonathan B. Postel Service Award posthumously to **Dave Taht** for his persistent work tackling bufferbloat, latency, and jitter (including FQ-CoDel and Cake) and his dedication to open-source software and mentorship. Frank Borsik accepted the award on Dave's behalf.

### 3. Leadership Reports

#### IESG Report (Roman Danyliw)
- **Process and Statements:** The IESG updated documentation to reflect modern practices regarding AD-sponsored documents and stream distinctions. One posting rights action was implemented. One appeal response was issued since IETF 126.
- **General Area WGs:** Updates were highlighted in `ianabiss` (IANA considerations) and `procon` (standards process updates). The Moderator Team published draft procedures for community review.
- **AI in Standardization:** 
  - A dedicated mailing list (`ai-in-standards`) was established.
  - Data analysis showed ~25% of mailing list traffic over the past three months contains AI-generated or AI-assisted content (ranging from 0% in some WGs to 80% in others).
  - 50% of new (-00) unadopted drafts over the past year were submitted by first-time authors (compared to 6–11% in previous years).
  - A rescheduled General Area session on AI measurement, use, and potential interventions was set for Friday (Session 4).

#### IAB Report (Dhruv Dhody)
- **Workshops & Statements:** Held a Post-Quantum Authentication Workshop in Prague (report pending) and published a statement on age-based restriction and online safety.
- **Liaisons & Outreach:** Participated in RIR/NOG events, ICANN, OpenSSL Conference, and African IGF. Responded to liaisons from ITU-T SG17 and sent a liaison to ICANN regarding the Root Server System Governance Proposal. Joint IAB/IESG liaison management procedures are being updated.
- **Community Support:** Announced the IAB Open session and the IAB New Work Help Desk.

#### IRTF Report (Dirk Kutscher)
- **Groups & Initiatives:** 12 of 17 Research Groups met during IETF 127. A new proposed research group, Net4AI (Networking for AI), held its first meeting.
- **Awards & Grants:** Applied Networking Research Prize (ANRP) talks were delivered by Muna Wang and Prateek. Four IRTF Diversity Travel Grants were awarded. IRSG is exploring initiatives to train future Internet researchers.

#### NomCom Report
- **2025 NomCom:** Completed the midterm selection of Jen Linkova as INT Area Director (confirmed by IAB).
- **2026 NomCom:** Interviewed 28 unique candidates. Feedback period closes Thursday at 12:00 PM local time prior to deliberations.

#### IETF Trust / IPMC Report (Wendy Seltzer)
- **Trust Dissolution & IPMC Transition:** Announced the official dissolution of the IETF Trust. All assets, trademarks, domain names, licenses, and IANA IPR have been successfully transferred to the IETF Intellectual Property Management Corporation (IPMC), a Delaware non-profit established in 2022.
- **Operations:** Handled eight licensing inquiries since the last report without requiring custom licenses or legal action. Document references and Technical/Legal Provisions (TLP) will be updated.

#### IETF LLC Operations & Board Reports (Jay Daley & Gonzalo Camarillo)
- **Sponsorship & Funding:** Recognized global hosts, meeting sponsors, and the Internet Society ($8M contribution projected for next year).
- **Consultations & Budget:** Community feedback is ongoing for the 2027 draft budget, RPC backlog options, and tools SLAs.
- **Financial Status:** Meeting revenues and expenses were both slightly below budget. Total investments included ~$2.4M in unrealized non-cash gains alongside endowment matching funds.
- **Board Activities:** Onboarded Pete Resnick as a new board member, updated the investment policy, and finalized a high-level LLC strategy document for upcoming public consultation.

